  "result": "The bug is fixed and all 8 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** in `shop/storage.py:17`, `save_order` loads the existing rows, filters them, adds the new order and writes the file back. The filter was backwards:\n\n```python\nrows = [r for r in self.load() if r[\"id\"] == order.id]   # before\nrows = [r for r in self.load() if r[\"id\"] != order.id]   # after\n```\n\nIt kept only rows with the **same** id as the order being saved, so every other order was deleted on each save. When you saved a second, different order, the first one was dropped and `load()` returned just one. With `!=`, it now drops only the old copy of this order and keeps all the others.\n\n**Tests:** there were no tests for `Store` before, so I added `tests/test_storage.py` with two:\n- Saving two different orders keeps both, which is the bug you reported.\n- Saving the same order again after changing its state leaves one row with the new state, so the fix doesn't create duplicates.\n\nThere's no `python` on this machine, so I ran the suite with `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .`.",
